    "culture": {
      "text": {
        "id": 45,
        "title": "14th review – Ava DuVernay takes on a bully president and his viper’s-nest court of Maga sycophants",
        "source": "The Guardian - Culture",
        "source_id": "guardian_culture",
        "category": "culture",
        "url": "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2026/nov/25/14th-review-ava-duvernay-takes-on-a-bully-president-and-his-vipers-nest-court-of-maga-sycophants",
        "published_at": "2026-11-25T13:00:26",
        "level": "C2",
        "slot_level": "C2",
        "source_level": "C2",
        "word_count": 407,
        "duration_sec": null,
        "body_text": "Ava DuVernay’s previous documentary 13th, energised by the Black Lives Matter movement, was a brilliant polemic about the 1th Amendment to the US constitution, which she argued perpetuated a link between slavery and prison for African American men.",
        "audio_url": null,
        "description": null,
        "license": "proprietary",
        "attribution": null,
        "truncated": true,
        "guid_hash": "60a3333c72b8c1e3e1f8753a380399c45c90be69",
        "summary": "DuVernay’s follow-up to her dissection of the 13th Amendment to the US constitution is a vehement and exhilarating defence of democracy Ava DuVernay’s previous documentary 13th , energised by the Black Lives Matter movement, was a brilliant polemic about the 1th Amendment to the US constitution , which she argued perpetuated a link between slavery and prison for African American men. Ten years later, and after an almost continuous decade of Trumpism, DuVernay has made a follow-up film about the 14th Amendment , and she has evidently arrived at a rather different attitude to the constitution and its authors’ intentions and good faith. Now she sees the enemy as Maga and the constitution as something that must be defended against a tinpot bully president and his viper’s-nest court of sycophants. The shift in view is not acknowledged, but the overall effect is similar. Utilising a gallery of very smart talking-head interviewees, including conservative Republicans, DuVernay has mounted a vehement and exhilarating defence of what might be called the second American Enlightenment, created by the 13th, 14th and 15th “reconstruction” amendments, drawn up between 1865 and 1870 after the American civil war. The 14th specifically established birthright citizenship, due process and equal rights for all races under the law, and furthermore barred insurrectionists from holding public office, all of which Trump appears casually intent on vandalising, airily amnesiac about his oath to uphold the constitution.",
        "origin": "fresh",
        "badges": [],
        "periods": [],
        "themes": [],
        "history_terms": [],
        "alt_text": "14th review – Ava DuVernay takes on a bully president and his viper’s-nest court of Maga sycophants — The Guardian - Culture",
